WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.411
Hi, I'm Onyx!

00:00:02.411 --> 00:00:07.271
Altar-1 is Aikido's security model, built to run inside a customer's own rack.

00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:08.018
Here's how.

00:00:09.892 --> 00:00:14.451
Take GLM-5.3, delete eighty-eight of its two hundred and fifty-six experts per

00:00:14.451 --> 00:00:17.491
layer, and store the rest at four bits.

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:25.358
It starts as GLM-5.3: seven hundred and fifty-three billion parameters at full precision.

00:00:25.778 --> 00:00:29.691
A quantised build stores the routed experts at four bits: four

00:00:29.691 --> 00:00:31.113
hundred and eighty-eight gigabytes.

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:36.238
A prune keeps a hundred and sixty-eight per layer, calibrated on Aikido's

00:00:36.238 --> 00:00:38.198
pentesting traces and multilingual text.

00:00:38.618 --> 00:00:41.415
The result is three hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes.

00:00:41.415 --> 00:00:45.611
Scales, and the parts never quantised, make that five point two four

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.360
bits a parameter, not four.

00:00:48.842 --> 00:00:52.777
In each expert layer, a router sends every token to a few experts.

00:00:53.197 --> 00:00:56.332
Pruning shrank the bank it picks from, to a hundred and sixty-eight.

00:00:57.454 --> 00:01:01.488
But it still picks eight: about forty billion active parameters, as before.

00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:03.841
A token costs no less to compute.

00:01:04.261 --> 00:01:07.946
What shrinks is memory: it fits on four H200 GPUs instead

00:01:07.946 --> 00:01:09.286
of six or more.

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:12.503
Which experts you keep matters too.

00:01:12.503 --> 00:01:16.576
At a hundred and ninety-two experts, the best domain plan drifts far

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.971
less than frequency picking, which is barely better than random.

00:01:21.092 --> 00:01:24.534
Altar matches its four-bit parent on thirty-one of thirty-two cases.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:29.010
But every case hides a known bug, so false alarms are never scored.

00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:34.125
Pruning experts costs prose roughly four times what it costs code, so

00:01:34.125 --> 00:01:36.830
a code-reading model is the right one to prune.

00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:43.921
To recap: fewer experts, four-bit weights, the same routing, nearly the same results.

00:01:44.341 --> 00:01:46.415
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:47.008
I'm Onyx.

00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:47.304
Bye!
